eated supply-chain disruptions and energy price pressures, sustaining U.S. inflation above the Fed’s 2% target and supporting market-implied odds of a 25-basis-point federal funds rate hike at the September 16-17 FOMC meeting. The July 28-29 decision left the target range unchanged at 3.50%-3.75% on a 9-3 vote, with three members dissenting in favor of an immediate increase amid solid economic growth and limited labor-market slack. Traders are now monitoring the August 12 CPI release and August 19 minutes for signals on whether supply shocks will keep a September move “firmly in play,” as priced by CME FedWatch futures near 50-60% probability. Historical precedent shows divided FOMC meetings often precede policy shifts when inflation data surprises to the upside.","context_requires_regen":true,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-12T13:33:00.482Z"},"version":"v1"}]
